WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.635 --> 00:00:02.550
You disrupted the
leadership campaign.

00:00:02.550 --> 00:00:05.110
You're disrupting
the mayoral campaign.

00:00:05.110 --> 00:00:06.741
You should be the
next prime minister.

00:00:06.741 --> 00:00:07.575
You're very sweet.

00:00:07.575 --> 00:00:09.193
They've left the centre.

00:00:09.193 --> 00:00:10.485
The parties have been captured.

00:00:12.180 --> 00:00:14.010
They're not coming back.

00:00:14.010 --> 00:00:16.650
How can two parties
possibly do justice

00:00:16.650 --> 00:00:17.780
to what modern Britain is?

00:00:17.780 --> 00:00:20.610
That sort of sense of
people wanting a disruption

00:00:20.610 --> 00:00:22.860
is palpable, I think.

00:00:22.860 --> 00:00:25.200
I've seen outsiders
go up in flames

00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:27.600
because they haven't
had a clue how

00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:29.907
the political system and
politics actually work.

00:00:29.907 --> 00:00:31.490
As an entrepreneur,
when I started out

00:00:31.490 --> 00:00:33.555
I got nothing but nice
things happen to me.

00:00:33.555 --> 00:00:34.930
Politics is
completely different.

00:00:34.930 --> 00:00:36.420
I can't say anything
nice about it.

00:00:36.420 --> 00:00:38.587
It is literally a viper's nest.

00:00:38.587 --> 00:00:40.680
Political disruptors
are tempting voters

00:00:40.680 --> 00:00:44.070
away from the UK's
two big main parties.

00:00:44.070 --> 00:00:47.460
Competition is fierce with
would-be radical options

00:00:47.460 --> 00:00:51.360
on the nationalist right and
even on the centre ground.

00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:54.070
Britain's politics
is being shaken up.

00:00:54.070 --> 00:00:56.340
And the result of
December's general election

00:00:56.340 --> 00:00:59.220
has never been so uncertain.

00:00:59.220 --> 00:01:04.709
The 2016 Brexit vote exploded
the usual left-right alignment.

00:01:04.709 --> 00:01:08.820
There are now at least four
parties battling it out.

00:01:08.820 --> 00:01:11.280
One of the insurgents,
the Brexit party,

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:15.240
is led by Nigel Farage, whose
lifelong dream for the UK

00:01:15.240 --> 00:01:18.870
to quit the EU may be
about to come true.

00:01:18.870 --> 00:01:23.070
There will be no Brexit
without the Brexit party.

00:01:23.070 --> 00:01:24.264
Of that, I'm certain.

00:01:25.665 --> 00:01:27.810
Brexit's thrown it all up
in the air because that

00:01:27.810 --> 00:01:29.340
went beyond left and right.

00:01:29.340 --> 00:01:30.930
And the political
parties haven't

00:01:30.930 --> 00:01:34.440
known how to react to a
major political decision that

00:01:34.440 --> 00:01:36.315
didn't fall under party lines.

00:01:36.315 --> 00:01:39.120
The other challengers
want to stop Brexit.

00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:42.495
But they also hope to
capitalise on the upheaval.

00:01:42.495 --> 00:01:46.920
Some form of tumult was probably
inevitable in British politics

00:01:46.920 --> 00:01:49.290
because whenever you go
through big societal change,

00:01:49.290 --> 00:01:50.695
you see tumult.

00:01:50.695 --> 00:01:53.400
Professor Jane Green, of
the British Election Study,

00:01:53.400 --> 00:01:57.360
maps and measures the UK's
changing voting patterns.

00:01:57.360 --> 00:02:04.260
We went to Oxford asked how
Britain went from this to this?

00:02:04.260 --> 00:02:06.960
From the 1960s through to
today we see more people

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:09.750
switching their vote between
general elections over time.

00:02:09.750 --> 00:02:12.140
So a much more fluid
volatile picture.

00:02:12.140 --> 00:02:14.550
So does this volatility
mean that it's

00:02:14.550 --> 00:02:16.320
kind of fertile territory?

00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:18.060
That there are
opportunities there

00:02:18.060 --> 00:02:20.445
for the political disruptors?

00:02:20.445 --> 00:02:22.410
You've got a very
available electorate.

00:02:22.410 --> 00:02:25.290
You've got opportunities
for the political parties.

00:02:25.290 --> 00:02:28.920
What you've also got is
loads of uncertainty.

00:02:28.920 --> 00:02:31.500
You can see there's a
disruption going on,

00:02:31.500 --> 00:02:33.510
and we don't know where
it's going to land.

00:02:33.510 --> 00:02:37.740
Claire Fox, a Libertarian from
the left was elected as an MEP

00:02:37.740 --> 00:02:41.760
for the Brexit party earlier
this year as voters deserted

00:02:41.760 --> 00:02:44.040
both Labour and the Tories.

00:02:44.040 --> 00:02:46.530
Of course, change is always
unpredictable, isn't it?

00:02:46.530 --> 00:02:47.640
Makes it scary.

00:02:47.640 --> 00:02:48.600
So is democracy.

00:02:48.600 --> 00:02:51.600
But to argue against
disruption on the basis

00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:56.040
of 'things worked' completely
misunderstands that for many

00:02:56.040 --> 00:02:57.405
people, they didn't.

00:02:57.405 --> 00:03:01.350
So we've talked a bit about
things being more up for grabs.

00:03:01.350 --> 00:03:02.100
Yeah.

00:03:02.100 --> 00:03:03.725
I'm going to try and
ask you to explain

00:03:03.725 --> 00:03:04.980
where the voters might be.

00:03:10.840 --> 00:03:15.080
So you think about one dimension
of politics from left to right.

00:03:15.080 --> 00:03:17.420
And within that
kind of left-right,

00:03:17.420 --> 00:03:20.660
bread and butter economic kind
of way of seeing the world,

00:03:20.660 --> 00:03:22.550
lots of people have
left of centre views.

00:03:22.550 --> 00:03:24.320
Lots people have
right of centre views,

00:03:24.320 --> 00:03:26.600
but the majority of people
would be in the middle.

00:03:26.600 --> 00:03:29.570
And therefore, it makes politics
very much about that kind

00:03:29.570 --> 00:03:32.300
of centre ground, about
competing for the majority

00:03:32.300 --> 00:03:33.785
of voters.

00:03:33.785 --> 00:03:36.740
Britain has always been about
understatement, compromise,

00:03:36.740 --> 00:03:38.420
pragmatism.

00:03:38.420 --> 00:03:40.280
And I think that's
where the energy is.

00:03:40.280 --> 00:03:42.425
I think it resonates deeply.

00:03:42.425 --> 00:03:44.690
Rory Stewart is
leaving parliament.

00:03:44.690 --> 00:03:46.610
He's left the
Conservative party.

00:03:46.610 --> 00:03:49.520
He wants to reinvent
moderate politics

00:03:49.520 --> 00:03:53.915
by standing as an independent
candidate for London mayor.

00:03:53.915 --> 00:03:57.230
I think actually
the UK's traditions

00:03:57.230 --> 00:03:59.455
are much more consensual,
much more designed

00:03:59.455 --> 00:04:03.130
for centre-ground politics than
almost anywhere in the world.

00:04:03.130 --> 00:04:06.890
That dimension is still very
important to voter choice now.

00:04:06.890 --> 00:04:09.710
But of course, we've all started
seeing the world predominately

00:04:09.710 --> 00:04:11.660
through the lens of Brexit.

00:04:11.660 --> 00:04:14.360
And Brexit isn't about bread
and butter left-right issues

00:04:14.360 --> 00:04:17.422
on the whole, it's about
this different dimension

00:04:17.422 --> 00:04:19.130
that cross cuts the
left-right dimension.

00:04:25.800 --> 00:04:29.865
It's divided the party, and
it's divided the voters.

00:04:29.865 --> 00:04:32.820
A lot of people who thought
of themselves at centre ground

00:04:32.820 --> 00:04:35.880
in the old politics, in the new
politics are far from centre

00:04:35.880 --> 00:04:36.600
ground.

00:04:36.600 --> 00:04:39.750
Chuka Umunna walked out of
Labour earlier this year,

00:04:39.750 --> 00:04:43.470
attempted to start a new
anti-Brexit centre party,

00:04:43.470 --> 00:04:48.000
Change UK, but is now trying
to redefine opposition politics

00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:52.290
from within the pro-European
Liberal Democrats.

00:04:52.290 --> 00:04:54.240
Those guys are no
longer centre ground.

00:04:54.240 --> 00:04:57.660
They are firmly on the liberal,
internationalist, open,

00:04:57.660 --> 00:05:01.167
anti-authoritarian side
of the new dichotomy.

00:05:01.167 --> 00:05:03.750
So when people say to me I want
a return to good centre ground

00:05:03.750 --> 00:05:06.375
policies, I'm kind of like, but
you're no longer centre ground.

00:05:06.375 --> 00:05:08.375
You are actually
firmly in one camp.

00:05:08.375 --> 00:05:09.750
Political scientists
like you are

00:05:09.750 --> 00:05:12.060
used to thinking about
voters in this rather more

00:05:12.060 --> 00:05:14.330
complicated way.

00:05:14.330 --> 00:05:16.380
In the past, we think
about it in terms

00:05:16.380 --> 00:05:19.020
of people that had more socially
conservative views and more

00:05:19.020 --> 00:05:20.640
socially liberal views.

00:05:20.640 --> 00:05:22.710
But also we're now
thinking much more

00:05:22.710 --> 00:05:25.710
about people that have
anti-immigration views and also

00:05:25.710 --> 00:05:30.360
pro-immigration views
and also anti-European or

00:05:30.360 --> 00:05:33.990
Brexit-supporting, Leave-voting
views or more pro-European,

00:05:33.990 --> 00:05:36.000
Remain-supporting views.

00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:37.978
And so we have the
impression that politics

00:05:37.978 --> 00:05:39.270
has become much more polarised.

00:05:40.620 --> 00:05:43.960
Both the Brexit party
and the radical Remainers

00:05:43.960 --> 00:05:47.265
are betting that politics
is now about values.

00:05:49.617 --> 00:05:51.450
There's different fault
lines, aren't there?

00:05:51.450 --> 00:05:54.990
So what's happened is rather
than saying the big decision

00:05:54.990 --> 00:05:57.600
in British politics today
is whether we nationalise

00:05:57.600 --> 00:06:00.540
the railways it's
actually our attitude

00:06:00.540 --> 00:06:02.005
to popular sovereignty.

00:06:02.005 --> 00:06:04.090
So you asked me the
question, where is the space?

00:06:04.090 --> 00:06:05.610
Yeah, where's the opportunity?

00:06:05.610 --> 00:06:08.920
So on the one hand, we talked
about kind of important.

00:06:08.920 --> 00:06:11.910
So if this issue
becomes less important,

00:06:11.910 --> 00:06:14.150
then we might worry
about left-right again.

00:06:14.150 --> 00:06:15.570
But what if this
issue, dimension,

00:06:15.570 --> 00:06:16.820
doesn't become less important?

00:06:16.820 --> 00:06:18.630
But at the current
time, it feels

00:06:18.630 --> 00:06:20.100
and looks in terms
of the evidence

00:06:20.100 --> 00:06:21.403
that people are pretty divided.

00:06:21.403 --> 00:06:23.070
If you look at some
of the people who've

00:06:23.070 --> 00:06:25.278
been running our country,
some of the decisions we've

00:06:25.278 --> 00:06:27.030
made in the last
decade or so, you

00:06:27.030 --> 00:06:31.590
go, how is such a brilliant
country in this mess?

00:06:31.590 --> 00:06:34.410
Simon Franks, once a
committed Labour party backer,

00:06:34.410 --> 00:06:36.930
is dismayed by
this polarisation.

00:06:36.930 --> 00:06:39.690
He's been spending time
and money trying to use his

00:06:39.690 --> 00:06:42.630
start-up skills to shake
up centre ground politics.

00:06:44.430 --> 00:06:48.390
The mission was to
scope out initially,

00:06:48.390 --> 00:06:50.820
is it possible to create a
new political party that could

00:06:50.820 --> 00:06:52.800
win in one electoral cycle?

00:06:52.800 --> 00:06:56.220
Can you, in politics, do
something that kind of maps

00:06:56.220 --> 00:06:59.430
entrepreneurialism
onto party politics?

00:06:59.430 --> 00:07:00.930
Yes, you can but,
not in the centre.

00:07:00.930 --> 00:07:02.722
If you're on the wings
of British politics,

00:07:02.722 --> 00:07:05.190
or in fact, any politics,
and you have a cause,

00:07:05.190 --> 00:07:07.560
you can mobilise people
incredibly quickly

00:07:07.560 --> 00:07:09.778
to bring about a
change because people

00:07:09.778 --> 00:07:11.570
are so desperate for
that change or believe

00:07:11.570 --> 00:07:13.260
so strongly in that cause.

00:07:13.260 --> 00:07:16.425
In the centre it's much harder
to do because, by definition,

00:07:16.425 --> 00:07:18.300
you should be more
balanced, more reasonable.

00:07:18.300 --> 00:07:19.980
You understand that
no one issue is

00:07:19.980 --> 00:07:22.440
going to make our country
completely better or completely

00:07:22.440 --> 00:07:23.235
worse.

00:07:23.235 --> 00:07:26.280
Josef Lentsch believes in
the power of the middle.

00:07:26.280 --> 00:07:30.390
This Austrian academic helped
start a successful new party

00:07:30.390 --> 00:07:33.210
and has written a book
on how to make it work.

00:07:33.210 --> 00:07:34.200
It's bloody hard.

00:07:34.200 --> 00:07:35.610
It's bloody hard
for politicians.

00:07:35.610 --> 00:07:39.600
These days, the political
itch to be scratched

00:07:39.600 --> 00:07:43.260
is that many people feel
not represented anymore.

00:07:43.260 --> 00:07:46.080
And too many of them
then decide to vote

00:07:46.080 --> 00:07:48.210
for populists and nationalists.

00:07:48.210 --> 00:07:50.880
But I think many of
them would actually

00:07:50.880 --> 00:07:52.980
like to have a choice
to vote for something

00:07:52.980 --> 00:07:54.840
different and constructive.

00:07:54.840 --> 00:07:59.430
The primary reason why Change
UK didn't succeed in the way

00:07:59.430 --> 00:08:03.130
that we would have liked
it to is, as you said,

00:08:03.130 --> 00:08:06.840
I'm not sure people were looking
for disruption in as much

00:08:06.840 --> 00:08:08.670
as they were looking
for their politics

00:08:08.670 --> 00:08:10.350
to be properly represented.

00:08:10.350 --> 00:08:12.660
But they weren't necessarily
precious about the vehicle

00:08:12.660 --> 00:08:14.160
through which you do that.

00:08:14.160 --> 00:08:16.260
And to try and
create something new

00:08:16.260 --> 00:08:19.830
in a non-presidential system
is nigh on impossible.

00:08:19.830 --> 00:08:22.950
In a sense you, can't just
compete on one dimension.

00:08:22.950 --> 00:08:25.140
People want to know
where you stand.

00:08:25.140 --> 00:08:26.850
So if you're competing
on this dimension,

00:08:26.850 --> 00:08:28.590
but you're divided
on this dimension

00:08:28.590 --> 00:08:30.298
because you've got
parties from the left,

00:08:30.298 --> 00:08:33.960
parties from the right,
then essentially, OK fine.

00:08:33.960 --> 00:08:36.809
So you've got this
bit sorted out.

00:08:36.809 --> 00:08:38.820
But are you over
here on the left?

00:08:38.820 --> 00:08:41.190
Or are you here on
the right in terms of

00:08:41.190 --> 00:08:42.937
where your voters
are likely to be?

00:08:42.937 --> 00:08:45.270
I think the most important
thing is if you want to build

00:08:45.270 --> 00:08:48.930
a centrist alternative, that
you're actually early on are

00:08:48.930 --> 00:08:51.750
starting to talk to the voters
and start to interlink what I

00:08:51.750 --> 00:08:53.640
call, 'islands of discontent.'

00:08:53.640 --> 00:08:56.230
Most political
start-ups will fail.

00:08:56.230 --> 00:08:57.450
I think that's not a problem.

00:08:57.450 --> 00:09:00.270
I think actually many, many
need to try for some of them

00:09:00.270 --> 00:09:02.040
to succeed.

00:09:02.040 --> 00:09:04.465
Once you've broken the
habits of a lifetime -

00:09:04.465 --> 00:09:06.840
at the European election,
obviously everything got thrown

00:09:06.840 --> 00:09:09.750
in the air - then
you're not quite that,

00:09:09.750 --> 00:09:11.370
we always vote
Labour in our family.

00:09:11.370 --> 00:09:13.530
We always vote
Tory in our family.

00:09:13.530 --> 00:09:14.400
Anything can happen.

00:09:14.400 --> 00:09:16.710
It's like when MPs rebel
against a whip, right?

00:09:16.710 --> 00:09:19.425
Once they get the taste for
it, it becomes possible again.

00:09:19.425 --> 00:09:22.470
Even those people who are
saying: let's get Brexit done,

00:09:22.470 --> 00:09:24.900
their argument is,
let's get Brexit done

00:09:24.900 --> 00:09:26.440
so we can go back to normal.

00:09:26.440 --> 00:09:30.180
And I think they underestimate
the appetite for a much more

00:09:30.180 --> 00:09:31.710
fundamental shift.

00:09:31.710 --> 00:09:34.695
Nothing's ever going
to go back, ever.

00:09:34.695 --> 00:09:38.340
The problem is that the
government of the centre has

00:09:38.340 --> 00:09:41.340
always seemed terribly sort
of bureaucratic and inert.

00:09:41.340 --> 00:09:42.850
It doesn't really
seem to listen.

00:09:42.850 --> 00:09:45.000
It doesn't seem to
engage, which gives people

00:09:45.000 --> 00:09:46.980
the idea that maybe
there's a silver bullet,

00:09:46.980 --> 00:09:49.930
maybe there's some
fantastic thing.

00:09:49.930 --> 00:09:51.022
And it's some character.

00:09:51.022 --> 00:09:51.545
An ideology.

00:09:51.545 --> 00:09:52.295
Yeah, an ideology.

00:09:52.295 --> 00:09:52.765
Or a person.

00:09:52.765 --> 00:09:53.280
Or a person.

00:09:53.280 --> 00:09:55.590
Like a hand grenade you
can chuck at the system,

00:09:55.590 --> 00:09:57.410
and the whole thing's
going to blow up.

00:09:57.410 --> 00:10:00.340
And suddenly, it's all
going to be much better.

00:10:00.340 --> 00:10:02.332
So there's no messiah coming.

00:10:02.332 --> 00:10:03.540
No, there can't be a messiah.

00:10:03.540 --> 00:10:05.020
I mean, I think I'm also...

00:10:05.020 --> 00:10:05.625
Not you.

00:10:05.625 --> 00:10:06.542
No, definitely not me.

00:10:06.542 --> 00:10:08.728
We've had conspicuous
examples of success

00:10:08.728 --> 00:10:10.020
on both the left and the right.

00:10:10.020 --> 00:10:12.660
I'm thinking of Nigel
Farage on one side,

00:10:12.660 --> 00:10:14.880
probably Labour's
Momentum on the other.

00:10:14.880 --> 00:10:17.580
But there's this whole space
in the centre with lots

00:10:17.580 --> 00:10:20.688
of plotting, lots of activity.

00:10:20.688 --> 00:10:22.605
But it's really hard to
make something happen.

00:10:22.605 --> 00:10:23.730
It's a much easier message.

00:10:23.730 --> 00:10:26.350
So Nigel Farage, who I think
is a brilliant communicator

00:10:26.350 --> 00:10:28.576
and I don't have this
disregard for him

00:10:28.576 --> 00:10:29.880
as so many people seem to have.

00:10:29.880 --> 00:10:31.890
I think he speaks
for a large community

00:10:31.890 --> 00:10:34.920
of our country about issues
that no one else will talk to.

00:10:34.920 --> 00:10:36.870
I think the same on the left.

00:10:36.870 --> 00:10:38.850
Jeremy Corbyn gets on
the stage and says,

00:10:38.850 --> 00:10:39.975
capitalists are bad people.

00:10:39.975 --> 00:10:42.267
The reason why your life
isn't as good as you'd like it

00:10:42.267 --> 00:10:43.950
is because of that
bunch over there.

00:10:43.950 --> 00:10:47.015
And some people, they go,
the messiah's arrived.

00:10:47.015 --> 00:10:48.850
No one's got a
monopoly on grievance.

00:10:48.850 --> 00:10:52.030
In the wake of the crash,
in the wake of austerity,

00:10:52.030 --> 00:10:55.150
in the wake of
globalisation, taking away

00:10:55.150 --> 00:10:57.550
the securities that
people took for granted,

00:10:57.550 --> 00:10:59.895
the question is, what
you do about that?

00:10:59.895 --> 00:11:02.680
We've completely failed to
produce a product that's

00:11:02.680 --> 00:11:04.150
really exciting.

00:11:04.150 --> 00:11:07.570
I mean, none of these
third-party centre party

00:11:07.570 --> 00:11:11.350
leaders have actually worked out
how to produce something that

00:11:11.350 --> 00:11:15.040
really makes the public think,
woo, well, OK, all right,

00:11:15.040 --> 00:11:19.975
actually, I'm not going to vote
the way that my parents voted.

00:11:19.975 --> 00:11:22.450
This election is not the end.

00:11:22.450 --> 00:11:24.310
And I think the most
important thing to note

00:11:24.310 --> 00:11:26.893
is this election's been called
in very peculiar circumstances.

00:11:26.893 --> 00:11:29.830
But I personally think that
the genie's out the bottle.

00:11:29.830 --> 00:11:34.000
And that what we are likely
to see in the next five years

00:11:34.000 --> 00:11:36.895
is a very disruptive
political scene.

00:11:36.895 --> 00:11:39.990
In the first winter
election for decades,

00:11:39.990 --> 00:11:43.990
established parties are being
buffeted from all sides.

00:11:43.990 --> 00:11:47.370
There are wide open spaces
in the political landscape

00:11:47.370 --> 00:11:51.580
and enormous potential
for storms to come.

00:11:51.580 --> 00:11:54.520
Jane, if you had
to put money on it,

00:11:54.520 --> 00:11:58.630
would you bet on political
insurgents, either a smaller

00:11:58.630 --> 00:12:03.040
party or a new party
trying to replace or split

00:12:03.040 --> 00:12:04.995
one of the two main parties?

00:12:04.995 --> 00:12:08.470
If the two mainstream parties
adapt their positions,

00:12:08.470 --> 00:12:10.810
working out kind
of where can they

00:12:10.810 --> 00:12:13.240
attract the majority of voters,
then it's very difficult,

00:12:13.240 --> 00:12:16.645
it's still very difficult for
minor parties to break through.

00:12:16.645 --> 00:12:19.000
In normal times, I
would say that it's

00:12:19.000 --> 00:12:23.330
impossible to actually get a
new party in the UK parliament.

00:12:23.330 --> 00:12:24.730
But we are not in normal times.

00:12:24.730 --> 00:12:25.780
We're past normal times.

00:12:25.780 --> 00:12:28.420
And I think, therefore,
don't give up hope.

00:12:28.420 --> 00:12:31.090
That there might be
something on the way.

00:12:31.090 --> 00:12:33.310
We can probably do better
if we think about it,

00:12:33.310 --> 00:12:36.280
redesign our political system,
reinvigorate our parties,

00:12:36.280 --> 00:12:38.060
maybe create some
new ones, maybe look

00:12:38.060 --> 00:12:40.700
at our voting system, look
the way we select MPs,

00:12:40.700 --> 00:12:42.235
look at the way treat MPs

00:12:42.235 --> 00:12:45.310
Anyone who believes that
everything goes back to normal

00:12:45.310 --> 00:12:47.430
is kidding themselves.

